#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:14
msgid ""
"#[cutile::entry()]\n"
"fn add<const S: [i32; 2]>(\n"
"    z: &mut Tensor<f32, S>,\n"
"    x: &Tensor<f32, { [-1, -1] }>,\n"
"    y: &Tensor<f32, { [-1, -1] }>,\n"
") {\n"
"    let tile_x = x.load_like(z);\n"
"    let tile_y = y.load_like(z);\n"
"    z.store(tile_x + tile_y);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:27
msgid ""
"The output tensor is mutable and already partitioned by the host launcher. "
"The input tensors are read-only and can be loaded into tiles that match the "
"output partition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:29
msgid "Tensor, Partition, Tile"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:31
msgid ""
"`Tensor<E, S>` is the device-side view of global memory. Kernels receive "
"tensors as parameters. A tensor can be loaded into a `Tile<E, S>` or used to "
"create a device-side partition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:33
msgid ""
"`Partition<E, S>` is a tiled view of a tensor. On the host, partitioning a "
"mutable tensor determines how many tile blocks launch and which region each "
"block writes. On the device, partitioning a read-only tensor lets a kernel "
"load arbitrary tiles by index."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:35
msgid ""
"`Tile<E, S>` is an immutable register-resident array fragment. Tile "
"operations create new tiles instead of mutating the original value:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:37
msgid ""
"let tile = x.load_like(z);\n"
"let shifted = tile + 1.0f32;\n"
"let scaled = shifted * 2.0f32;\n"
"z.store(scaled);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:44
msgid ""
"![Data flow: Load from Tensor to Tile, Compute in registers, Store back to "
"Tensor](../_static/images/data-flow.svg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:44
msgid ""
"Data flow: Load from Tensor to Tile, Compute in registers, Store back to "
"Tensor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:46
msgid "Tensor -> Partition -> Tile -> Compute -> Store\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:50
msgid "Partitioning and the Grid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:52
msgid "Mutable outputs are partitioned on the host before launch:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:54
msgid ""
"let mut z = api::zeros::<f32>(&[1024, 1024]).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
"let _ = add((&mut z).partition([64, 64]), &x, &y).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:59
msgid ""
"The partition shape becomes the static shape seen by the kernel. A `[1024, "
"1024]` tensor partitioned as `[64, 64]` creates a logical grid of `(16, 16, "
"1)` tile blocks. Each tile block receives one writable sub-tensor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:61
msgid ""
"Read-only inputs do not need host-side partitioning. Inside the kernel, "
"partition them with the shape needed by the algorithm:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:63
msgid ""
"let pid_m = program_id(0);\n"
"let part_x = x.partition(shape![BM, BK]);\n"
"let tile_x = part_x.load([pid_m, i]);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:69
msgid ""
"The same read-only tensor can be partitioned multiple ways inside one "
"kernel. This is common in matrix multiplication, where the left-hand side "
"and right-hand side are loaded with different tile shapes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:71
msgid ""
"The launch grid is inferred from mutable output partitions unless the "
"launcher sets it explicitly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:73
msgid "kernel(z.partition([64, 64]), x).grid((16, 16, 1)).sync_on(&stream)?;\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:77
msgid ""
"When a kernel has multiple mutable outputs, their inferred grids must match."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:79
msgid "Static and Dynamic Shapes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:81
msgid ""
"Static dimensions are compile-time constants. Dynamic dimensions use `-1` "
"and are resolved from the runtime tensor shape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:83
msgid ""
"#[cutile::entry()]\n"
"fn normalize<const S: [i32; 2]>(\n"
"    z: &mut Tensor<f32, S>,          // Static tile shape from partition.\n"
"    x: &Tensor<f32, { [-1, -1] }>,   // Runtime full tensor shape.\n"
") {\n"
"    let tile = x.load_like(z);\n"
"    z.store(tile);\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:94
msgid ""
"Static shapes let the compiler check operations and optimize layout. Dynamic "
"dimensions let the same compiled variant handle different full tensor sizes. "
"The common pattern is static output tile shape and dynamic read-only input "
"shape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:96
msgid ""
"Const generic arrays such as `const S: [i32; 2]` and `shape![BM, BK]` carry "
"tile dimensions through the type system. Changing a const generic value can "
"create a new compiled variant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:98
msgid "Loading, Computing, Storing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:100
msgid ""
"`input.load_like(output)` loads a read-only tensor region matching the "
"output tensor's tile shape and tile-block coordinates. For explicit device-"
"side partitions, call `partition.load(index)`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:102
msgid ""
"let part_x = x.partition(shape![BM, BK]);\n"
"let tile_x = part_x.load([pid_m, k_tile]);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:107
msgid "Writable tensors store tile results:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:109
msgid ""
"let result = tile_x + tile_y;\n"
"z.store(result);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:114
msgid ""
"Use `load_tile_mut` when a kernel needs to read the existing output value "
"before writing a new one:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../guide/tensors-and-tiles.md:116
msgid ""
"let acc = load_tile_mut(z);\n"
"z.store(acc + update);\n"
msgstr ""

